dist
dist-ssr

# fallow (dead-code analysis): cache + generated SARIF. The committed baseline
# under fallow-baselines/ is intentionally tracked and must NOT be ignored.
.fallow/
*.sarif

# Image-optimisation manifest written by scripts/build-docs-index.js. Nothing is gained by
# tracking it: the cache key is `mtimeMs-size` and git does not preserve mtimes, so a
# committed copy can never hit on a fresh checkout. (It does not currently hit locally
# either - copyFiles() runs once per corpus and each call overwrites the whole manifest, so
# the public-docs entries are destroyed by the internal-docs pass on every run. Tracked
# separately; that is a bug in the script, not a reason to commit its output.)
# What tracking it *did* do is churn: the script runs in `make verify` and the pre-commit
# hook, so it is rewritten constantly and rode along into unrelated commits - a TFE
# run-tasks feature, a dependency batch, a GCP OIDC feature and a docs audit each carry a
# spurious one-line diff to it.
# The optimised binaries in .docs-image-cache/ stay tracked on purpose: those are
# deterministic for a given source image, so they do not churn.
.image-cache.json
test-results/
*.local

